  "reasoning": "Step 1: Identify key symptoms - The patient reports fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, fatigue, and difficulty concentrating secondary to the fever.\nStep 2: Evaluate systemic vs localized signs - Fever, headache, and fatigue are systemic symptoms indicating a whole-body response, likely viral. Swollen lymph nodes suggest an active immune response to an infection, commonly in the head/neck region.\nStep 3: Rule out other conditions - The absence of rashes/blistering rules out chicken pox, dengue, and fungal infections. Lack of joint pain/stiffness rules out arthritis. No breathing difficulties rule out asthma and pneumonia. No digestive or urinary complaints rule out typhoid, peptic ulcer, and UTI. No visual disturbances or one-sided severe pain rules out migraine and hypertension.\nStep 4: Match with remaining options - The combination of fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, and profound fatigue is a classic presentation of a viral upper respiratory infection. Among the available choices, this aligns perfectly with a common cold.",
